// Package narrated is a file with more narration than code. It is committed
// broken on purpose and must never be tidied: it is how the ceiling is shown
// biting.
//
// The paragraphs below say the same thing several times over, which is the
// defect the ceiling exists to name. None of it tells a reader anything the
// three lines of code do not already say, and that is the point.
//
// A comment earns its place by saying WHY. A file where prose outweighs code is
// not documented, it is narrated, and the reader has to hold both the code and
// somebody's essay about the code in their head at once.
package narrated

// Total adds two integers together.
//
// It takes the first integer and the second integer and returns their sum. The
// sum of two integers is the integer you get when you add them. Addition is
// commutative, so the order of the arguments does not matter here.
//
// This restatement is the shape the gate refuses. The signature already said
// all of it, twice.
func Total(a, b int) int {
	return a + b
}

// Twice doubles an integer.
//
// It multiplies the integer by two. Multiplying by two is the same as adding
// the integer to itself. Either formulation returns the same answer for every
// integer that does not overflow, and overflow is not handled here.
//
// The comment is longer than the function and says less.
func Twice(n int) int {
	return n * 2
}
